ofound. So esteemed is calculus that its name is often preceded by “the,” as in von Neumann’s observation above....\n\n  6. Newton\n\nCHAPTER 1 Newton (pp. 5-19)\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt1287kvm.6\nCHAPTER 12 Volterra (pp. 170-182)\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt1287kvm.17\n\nVito Volterra (1860–1940) flourished alongside a number of Italian mathematicians in the second half of the nineteenth century. Like his countrymen Giuseppe Peano (1858–1932), Eugenio Beltrami (1835–1900), and Ulisse Dini (1845–1918), he left his mark, contributing to applied areas like electrostatics and fluid dynamics, as well as to theoretical ones like mathematical analysis. It is of course the last of these that we consider here.\n\nAlthough born on the Adriatic coast, Volterra was raised in Florence, the epicenter of the Italian Renaissance. He walked the same streets as had Michelangelo and attended schools named after Dante...\n\n  18. Baire\n\nCHAPTER 13 Baire (pp. 183-199)\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt1287kvm.18\n\nIn his doctoral thesis of 1899, René Baire (1874–1932) assessed the importance of set theory to mathematical analysis:\n\nOne can even say, in a general manner, that ... any problem relative to the theory of functions leads to certain questions relative to the theory of sets and, insofar as these latter questions are or can be addressed, it is possible to resolve, more or less completely, the given problem [1].\n\nAs we shall see, Baire not only advocated this position but did a splendid job of practicing it.\n\nUnfortunately, his mathematical triumphs were confined to the brief periods when...\n\n  19. Lebesgue\n\nCHAPTER 14 Lebesgue (pp. 200-219)\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt1287kvm.19\n\nAs the nineteenth century became the twentieth, mathematicians had reason to congratulate themselves. The calculus had been around for over two centuries. Its foundations were no longer suspect, and many of its open questions had been resolved. Analysis had come a long way since the early days of Newton and Leibniz.\n\nThen Henri Lebesgue (1875–1941) entered the picture. He was a brilliant doctoral student at the Sorbonne when, in 1902, he revolutionized integration theory and, by extension, real analysis itself. He did so with a dissertation that has been described as “one of the finest which any mathematician has...\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCHAPTER 2. Leibniz (https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9780691184548-006/html)\nciteturn981search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # CHAPTER 2. ...   * [Button: William Dunham ] ... The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue, Princeton: Princeton University Press, 2018, pp.\n\nKapitel\n\nImage: Cite this Zitieren\n\n# CHAPTER 2. Leibniz\n\n  * [Button: William Dunham ]\n\nDunham, William. \"CHAPTER 2. Leibniz\". The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue, Princeton: Princeton University Press, 2018, pp. 20-34. https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691184548-006\n\nDunham, W. (2018). CHAPTER 2. Leibniz. In The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue (pp. 20-34). Princeton: Princeton University Press. https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691184548-006\n\nDunham, W. 2018. CHAPTER 2. Leibniz. The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue. Princeton: Princeton University Press, pp. 20-34. https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691184548-006\n\nDunham, William. \"CHAPTER 2. Leibniz\" In The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue, 20-34. Princeton: Princeton University Press, 2018. https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691184548-006\n\n\n© 2018 Princeton University Press, Princeton\n\nSie haben derzeit keinen Zugang zu diesem Inhalt.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue | JSTOR (https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv3dnpr8)\nciteturn981search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; Image: Cover of The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue ... WILLIAM DUNHAM With a new preface by the author ... “The calculus,” wrote John von Neumann (1903–1957), “was the first achievement of modern mathematics, and it is difficult to overestimate its importance” [1]. ... In his doctoral thesis of 1899, René Baire (1874–1932) assessed the importance of set theory to mathematical analysis: ... CHAPTER 14 Lebesgue (pp. ... He was a brilliant doctoral student at the Sorbonne when, in 1902, he revolutionized integration theory and, by extension, real analysis itself.\n\nImage: Cover of The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue\n\nThe Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue\n\nWILLIAM DUNHAM With a new preface by the author\n\nSeries: Princeton Science Library\n\nCopyright Date: 2018\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctv3dnpr8\n\nEdition: NED - New edition\n\nPublished by: Princeton University Press\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv3dnpr8\n\nSearch for reviews of this book\n\nBook Info\n\nDescription\n\nMore than three centuries after its creation, calculus remains a dazzling intellectual achievement and the gateway to higher mathematics. This book charts its growth and development by sampling from the work of some of its foremost practitioners, beginning with Isaac Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz in the late seventeenth century and continuing to Henri Lebesgue at the dawn of the twentieth. Now with a new preface by the author, this book documents the evolution of calculus from a powerful but logically chaotic subject into one whose foundations are thorough, rigorous, and unflinching—a story of genius triumphing over some of the toughest, subtlest problems imaginable. In touring The Calculus Gallery, we can see how it all came to be.\n\nEISBN\n\n978-0-691-18454-8\n\n0-691-18454-2\n\nSubjects Mathematics\n\nView Accessibility Metadata\n\nTable of Contents\n\nSelect all\n\n