ess, pp. 52-68. https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691184548-008\n\nDunham, William. \"CHAPTER 4. Euler\" In The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue, 52-68. Princeton: Princeton University Press, 2018. https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691184548-008\n\nDunham W. CHAPTER 4. Euler. In: The Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Calculus Gallery Chapter Summary | William Dunham (https://www.bookey.app/book/the-calculus-gallery)\nciteturn981search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; In \"The Calculus Gallery,\" William Dunham dives into the rich history of calculus, showcasing its evolution over three centuries as a monumental intellectual achievement and essential pathway to advanced mathematics. ... Introduction to Lebesgue's Contributions  | Transitioning from the 19th to 20th century, Henri Lebesgue revolutionized integration theory with his 1902 dissertation, prompting a re-evaluation of real analysis. ... Notable developments included: - Newton's binomial expansions into infinite series (1669) and Cauchy’s convergence criteria (1827). - Euler's basic differential calculations (1755) and Baire's continuity properties of derivatives (1899). - Leibniz's area calculations through the transmutation theorem (1691) and Lebesgue's integral theory (1904). ... Summary of Chapter 16: The Calculus Gallery by William Dunham Introduction This chapter covers significant historical figures and milestones in the development of calculus, highlighting key contributions from mathematicians such as Newton, Leibniz, the Bernoullis, Euler, Cauchy, and Riemann.\nConclusion Baire's work on the category theorem and his classification of functions significantly amplified the understanding of continuity, set theory, and mathematical analysis at the turn of the twentieth century. His legacy continues to influence modern mathematics, emphasizing the profound interplay between functions and sets.\n\n### Chapter 14 | Lebesgue\n\nCHAPTER 14: Lebesgue Introduction to Lebesgue's Contributions - As we transitioned from the 19th to the 20th century, calculus became well-established with many issues resolved. However, Henri Lebesgue revolutionized integration theory through his dissertation in 1902, prompting a re-evaluation of real analysis. Riemann Integral and Its Limitations - Previous discussions outlined flaws in the Riemann integral, notably requiring additional hypotheses for validity. Examples illustrated how certain functions could be integrable pointwise yet lead to non-integrable limits, revealing deeper issues with Riemann's approach. Lebesgue Measure and Measure Zero - In his monograph from 1904, Lebesgue introduced a method of measuring sets via \"length,\" proposing the notion of \"measure zero.\" --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Calculus Gallery: Masterpieces from Newton to Lebesgue - William Dunham - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Calculus_Gallery.html?id=7XOYDwAAQBAJ)\nciteturn981search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; a₁ algebraic numbers analysis Augustin-Louis Cauchy b₁ Baire category theorem bd bd bd bd³ Bernoulli bounded category set Cauchy Cauchy's chapter consider continuous functions curve defined definition dense set denumerable derivative differential calculus Dirichlet's function discontinuous functions equation Euler example exists Figure finite follows function f Georg Cantor graph harmonic series Henri Lebesgue Ibid ideas inequalities infinite series infinitely small integer coefficients intermediate value intermediate value theorem intuition irrational Jakob Johann Lebesgue integral Lebesgue's Leibniz series lemma length lim f(x Liouville Liouville's mathematical mathematicians measure zero modern Newton notation nowhere-dense sets open interval Opera omnia original oscillation outer content P₁ partition pathological function pointwise discontinuous pointwise limit polynomial proof proved quantities real numbers René Baire result Riemann integrable ruler function subinterval transcendental value theorem Vito Volterra Volterra Weierstrass whole number x₁\n\nPage 35\n\nPage 20\n\n### Contents\n\n00Dunham_intro 14  | 1\n | \n01Dunham_ch01 519  | 5\n | \n02Dunham_ch02 2034  | 20\n | \n03Dunham_ch03 3551  | 35\n | \n04Dunham_ch04 5268  | 52\n | \n05Dunham_ch05 6975  | 69\n | \n06Dunham_ch06 7695  | 76\n | \n07Dunham_ch07 96115  | 96\n | \n\n10Dunham_ch10 149157  | 149\n | \n11Dunham_ch11 158169  | 158\n | \n12Dunham_ch12 170182  | 170\n | \n13Dunham_ch13 183199  | 183\n | \n14Dunham_ch14 200219_REVISED  | 200\n | \n15Dunham_aftrwrd 220222  | 220\n | \n16Dunham_notes 223232  | 223\n | \n17Dunham_Index 233236  | 233\n\n08Dunham_ch08 116127  | 116\n | \n09Dunham_ch09 128148  | 128\n | \n\n\n### Common terms and phrases\n\na₁ algebraic numbers analysis Augustin-Louis Cauchy b₁ Baire category theorem bd bd bd bd³ Bernoulli bounded category set Cauchy Cauchy's chapter consider continuous functions curve defined definition dense set denumerable derivative differential calculus Dirichlet's function discontinuous functions equation Euler example exists Figure finite follows function f Georg Cantor graph harmonic series Henri Lebesgue Ibid ideas inequalities infinite series infinitely small integer coefficients intermediate value intermediate value theorem intuition irrational Jakob Johann Lebesgue integral Lebesgue's Leibniz series lemma length lim f(x Liouville Liouville's mathematical mathematicians measure zero modern Newton notation nowhere-dense sets open interval Opera omnia original oscillation outer content P₁ partition pathological function pointwise discontinuous pointwise limit polynomial proof proved quantities real numbers René Baire result Riemann integrable ruler function subinterval transcendental value theorem Vito Volterra Volterra Weierstrass whole number x₁\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Calculus Gallery (https://campusstore.miamioh.edu/calculus-gallery-dunham-william/bk/9780691095653)\nciteturn981search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; William Dunham lucidly presents the definitions, theorems, and proofs. ... Like a great museum,The Calculus Galleryis filled with masterpieces, among which are Bernoulli's early attack upon the harmonic series (1689), Euler's brilliant approximation of pi (1779), Cauchy's classic proof of the fundamental theorem of calculus (1823), Weierstrass's mind-boggling counterexample (1872), and Baire's original \"category theorem\" (1899). ... In touringThe Calculus Gallery, we can see how it all came to be. ... CHAPTER 14 Lebesgue  |  | 200  | (20)\nWilliam Dunham lucidly presents the definitions, theorems, and proofs. \"Students of literature read Shakespeare; students of music listen to Bach,\" he writes. But this tradition of studying the major works of the \"masters\" is, if not wholly absent, certainly uncommon in mathematics. This book seeks to redress that situation. Like a great museum,The Calculus Galleryis filled with masterpieces, among which are Bernoulli's early attack upon the harmonic series (1689), Euler's brilliant approximation of pi (1779), Cauchy's classic proof of the fundamental theorem of calculus (1823), Weierstrass's mind-boggling counterexample (1872), and Baire's original \"category theorem\" (1899). Collectively, these selections document the evolution of calculus from a powerful but logically chaotic subject into one whose foundations are thorough, rigorous, and unflinching--a story of genius triumphing over some of the toughest, most subtle problems imaginable. Anyone who has studied and enjoyed calculus will discover in these pages the sheer excitement each mathematician must have felt when pushing into the unknown. In touringThe Calculus Gallery, we can see how it all came to be.